    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d62">

                <pb facs="0115" n="97" xml:id="pg97"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">843.7962/9–2547</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Canada</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Atherton</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">restricted</hi>
                        <lb/> No. 5519</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ottawa</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-09-25">September 25,
                        1947</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to the
                    Department’s Restricted telegram No. 161 of September 22, 1947, 5 p.m., and to
                    the Embassy’s reply No. 141 of September 24, 2 p.m.,<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d62-f1"/> both on the subject of the Newfoundland Air Bases
                    Agreement.</p>
                <p>In accordance with the promise made by the Department of External Affairs, as
                    mentioned in the Embassy’s telegram, there is enclosed herewith copy of an <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Aide-Mémoire</hi> dated September 25, 1947, which is
                    entitled “Newfoundland-United States Base Fields Agreement”, which the
                    Department of External Affairs has transmitted to the Canadian High Commissioner
                    in St. John’s, Newfoundland, for delivery to the Newfoundland Commission of
                    Government. The Embassy believes that this <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Aide-Mémoire</hi> goes as far as it is possible for the Canadian Government
                    to go, particularly in view of the present very delicate relationships which
                    exist between the Dominion and Newfoundland over confederation.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>For the Ambassador <lb/>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Julian</hi> F. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Harrington</hi>
                    </signed>
                    <lb/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">American Minister</hi>
                </closer>


                <xi:include href="d62-a1.xml" xpointer="d62-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
